  "prediction": "The report outlines the operations of the Unemployment Trust Fund (UTF) and the procedures for states to borrow from it when they run out of funds to pay unemployment benefits. The UTF is financed through unemployment taxes paid by employers and is managed by the US Department of Labor. When a state's unemployment trust fund runs out of money, the governor can request a loan from the UTF. The loan is interest-free if repaid by September 30 of the same year. However, if the loan is not repaid by this date, it accrues interest at a rate determined by the US Treasury.\n\nThe report also discusses the potential consequences for states that fail to repay their loans. These consequences include a reduction in the federal unemployment tax credit, which can increase the amount of federal unemployment taxes that employers in the state have to pay. The report provides information on the number of states that had outstanding loans and the amount of accrued interest payments as of FY2019. At that time, only the US Virgin Islands had an outstanding loan. The report also explains the conditions that states must meet to be eligible for interest-free loans from the UTF. These conditions include having had an Average High Cost (AHCM) of at least 1.0 in at least one of the past five years and maintaining a certain level of unemployment tax collection in the year of the loan and the previous year.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the operations of the Unemployment Trust Fund (UTF) and the procedures for states to borrow from it when they run out of funds to pay unemployment benefits. The UTF is financed through unemployment taxes paid by employers and is managed by the US Department of Labor. When a state's unemployment trust fund runs out of money, the governor can request a loan from the UTF. The loan is interest-free if repaid by September 30 of the same year. However, if the loan is not repaid by this date, it accrues interest at a rate determined by the US Treasury.\n\nThe report also discusses the potential consequences for states that fail to repay their loans. These consequences include a reduction in the federal unemployment tax credit, which can increase the amount of federal unemployment taxes that employers in the state have to pay. The report provides information on the number of states that had outstanding loans and the amount of accrued interest payments as of FY2019. At that time, only the US Virgin Islands had an outstanding loan. The report also explains the conditions that states must meet to be eligible for interest-free loans from the UTF. These conditions include having had an Average High Cost (AHCM) of at least 1.0 in at least one of the past five years and maintaining a certain level of unemployment tax collection in the year of the loan and the previous year.",
  "score": 37.39130385240076,
  "answers": [
    "Although states have a great deal of autonomy in how they establish and run their unemployment insurance programs, federal law requires states to pay Unemployment Compensation (UC) benefits promptly as provided under state law. During some recessions, current taxes and reserve balances may be insufficient to cover state obligations for UC benefits. States may borrow funds from the federal loan account within the Unemployment Trust Fund (UTF) to meet UC benefit obligations. This report summarizes how insolvent states may borrow funds from the UTF loan account to meet their UC benefit obligations. It includes the manner in which states must repay federal UTF loans. It also provides details on how the UTF loans may trigger potential interest accrual and explains the timetable for increased net Federal Unemployment Taxes Act (FUTA) taxes if the funds are not repaid promptly. Outstanding loans listed by state may be found at the Department of Labor's (DOL's) website, https://oui.doleta.gov/unemploy/budget.asp."
  ],
